<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Filippo Brunelleschi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1377">1377</a>–<a href="/years/1446">1446</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Filippo Brunelleschi (1377–1446) was a Florentine architect and engineer, regarded as a founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> architecture. His dome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a>, built from 1420 to 1436 without wooden centring, remains the largest masonry dome in the world, and his Ospedale degli Innocenti and church of San Lorenzo revived classical forms. He is also credited with the first demonstration of linear perspective.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi.jpg" alt="Filippo Brunelleschi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: scan uploaded by Sailko, cropped by MenkinAlRire · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masaccio,_cappella_brancacci,_san_pietro_in_cattedra._ritratto_di_filippo_brunelleschi-184.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Brunelleschi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1377, the son of Brunellesco di Lippo Lapi, a notary and civil servant, and Giuliana Spini. He received a literary and mathematical education, but being artistically inclined he was apprenticed at 15 as a goldsmith and sculptor in cast bronze, and in December 1398 he became a master.</p><p>In 1401 he was one of seven competitors for the second pair of bronze doors of the Florence Baptistery, each making a panel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac">Sacrifice of Isaac</a>. The jury could not choose between his panel and that of Lorenzo Ghiberti and proposed that the two collaborate. Brunelleschi refused to give up total control and preferred that the work go to Ghiberti. He later gave up sculpture for architecture and optics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first architectural commission was the Ospedale degli Innocenti, a foundling hospital begun in 1419 and opened on 25 January 1445, whose loggia drew clearly on classical forms and became a model across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He also designed the Old Sacristy and the nave of the Basilica of San Lorenzo for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">Medici family</a>.</p><p>In 1418 he won the competition to build the dome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a>, helped by a brick model made by his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a>. His later works included the Pazzi Chapel, the Basilica of Santo Spirito and Santa Maria degli Angeli, an octagonal church whose funds the government seized in 1437 for a war against Lucca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The dome was built from 1420 to 1436 with two shells, one within the other, and more than four million bricks. Brunelleschi left no plans of it, and he invented a new hoisting machine and chains of wood and sandstone that act as tension rings. His early biographer Antonio Manetti says that between 1415 and 1420 he made perspective paintings of the Baptistery and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-vecchio">Palazzo Vecchio</a>, and he is credited as the first to describe a precise system of linear perspective. In 1421 he received what is thought to be one of the first modern patents, for a boat to carry goods on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>. He died on 15 April 1446 and was buried in the crypt of the cathedral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Filippo Brunelleschi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the fifteenth century <b>Filippo Brunelleschi</b>’s dome of Florence Cathedral, completed in 1436, his use of linear perspective, the sculpture of Donatello and the painting of Masaccio launched the artistic Renaissance, followed by Sandro Botticelli, Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo, whose David was set up outside the Palazzo…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Filippo Brunelleschi</b> was among the architects who looked to the building for inspiration.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1421" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Filippo Brunelleschi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1421">What was here in 1421? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
